1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,275
Two kids balance a see-saw.

2
00:00:02,275 --> 00:00:04,308
A beam balance comes to rest.

3
00:00:04,308 --> 00:00:06,390
A crowbar lifts a heavy stone.

4
00:00:06,790 --> 00:00:09,967
Each one is about the same idea: balance.

5
00:00:10,417 --> 00:00:14,037
At the park, Diya sits on a see-saw
with a friend twice as heavy.

6
00:00:14,387 --> 00:00:15,862
Both sit at the ends.

7
00:00:15,862 --> 00:00:18,230
Her side shoots up, and stays there.

8
00:00:18,230 --> 00:00:21,308
Where must the heavy friend
sit, so they balance?

9
00:00:21,808 --> 00:00:24,804
To answer that, we need
the rules of equilibrium.

10
00:00:25,404 --> 00:00:29,935
A rigid body is in equilibrium when
nothing about its motion changes.

11
00:00:30,285 --> 00:00:33,172
First, its centre of
mass does not speed up.

12
00:00:33,172 --> 00:00:35,630
That needs the total force to be zero.

13
00:00:35,980 --> 00:00:38,616
Second, it does not start to turn faster.

14
00:00:38,616 --> 00:00:41,069
That needs the total torque to be zero.

15
00:00:41,469 --> 00:00:43,072
Both rules at once.

16
00:00:43,072 --> 00:00:45,354
Zero force, and zero torque.

17
00:00:45,954 --> 00:00:48,058
Our motion notebook, side view.

18
00:00:48,058 --> 00:00:52,190
A light see-saw, pivoted at the
middle, two metres on each side.

19
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:55,379
Diya, thirty kilograms,
sits at the right end.

20
00:00:55,379 --> 00:00:58,445
Her friend, sixty kilograms,
sits at the left end.

21
00:00:58,845 --> 00:01:01,232
The friend's weight
turns it anticlockwise.

22
00:01:01,232 --> 00:01:03,126
Diya's weight turns it clockwise.

23
00:01:03,126 --> 00:01:05,847
The friend wins, so the
left side goes down.

24
00:01:06,247 --> 00:01:07,308
Slow-mo replay.

25
00:01:07,308 --> 00:01:08,687
Freeze as it tips.

26
00:01:09,037 --> 00:01:12,280
Each weight times its
lever arm gives a torque.

27
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:15,533
Sixty g times two is one
hundred and twenty g.

28
00:01:15,533 --> 00:01:18,095
Thirty g times two is only sixty g.

29
00:01:18,495 --> 00:01:20,296
Now the friend moves in.

30
00:01:20,296 --> 00:01:23,527
At one point five metres,
it still tips left.

31
00:01:23,927 --> 00:01:26,468
At one metre, the two torques match.

32
00:01:26,468 --> 00:01:29,108
Sixty times one equals thirty times two.

33
00:01:29,108 --> 00:01:32,568
The net torque is zero, and
the see-saw stays level.

34
00:01:33,168 --> 00:01:37,562
On the graph, the net torque falls in
a straight line as the friend moves

35
00:01:37,562 --> 00:01:39,871
in, and crosses zero at one metre.

36
00:01:40,471 --> 00:01:43,103
Let's write the rules, one step at a time.

37
00:01:43,453 --> 00:01:44,353
Step one.

38
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:48,706
Translational equilibrium: the
vector sum of all forces is zero.

39
00:01:49,456 --> 00:01:50,356
Step two.

40
00:01:50,292 --> 00:01:54,948
Rotational equilibrium: the vector
sum of all torques is zero, about any

41
00:01:54,948 --> 00:01:55,848
point.

42
00:01:56,319 --> 00:01:57,263
Step three.

43
00:01:57,263 --> 00:02:00,381
For the see-saw, the
pivot pushes up with N.

44
00:02:00,381 --> 00:02:03,551
So N equals the two
weights added: ninety g.

45
00:02:04,301 --> 00:02:05,301
Step four.

46
00:02:05,301 --> 00:02:09,816
Torques about the pivot: W
one d one equals W two d two.

47
00:02:09,816 --> 00:02:12,090
Anticlockwise equals clockwise.

48
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:13,740
Step five.

49
00:02:13,624 --> 00:02:15,662
This is the principle of moments.

50
00:02:15,662 --> 00:02:19,510
For a lever, load times load arm
equals effort times effort arm.

51
00:02:20,260 --> 00:02:21,372
Remember this.

52
00:02:21,372 --> 00:02:25,310
Equilibrium needs zero total
force, and zero total torque.

53
00:02:26,010 --> 00:02:28,196
So where does the heavy friend sit?

54
00:02:28,196 --> 00:02:29,172
Link by link.

55
00:02:29,522 --> 00:02:31,898
Diya's torque is thirty g times two.

56
00:02:31,898 --> 00:02:33,056
Sixty g metres.

57
00:02:33,456 --> 00:02:37,850
The friend weighs twice as much,
so needs only half the lever arm.

58
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:40,319
Half of two metres is one metre.

59
00:02:40,319 --> 00:02:42,944
Twice the weight sits
at half the distance.

60
00:02:43,544 --> 00:02:44,539
Now you try.

61
00:02:44,539 --> 00:02:47,223
They balance at one metre and two metres.

62
00:02:47,223 --> 00:02:48,710
Both move halfway in.

63
00:02:48,710 --> 00:02:50,245
Does it still balance?

64
00:02:50,245 --> 00:02:51,481
Pause and decide.

65
00:02:54,581 --> 00:02:55,481
Yes!

66
00:02:55,047 --> 00:02:57,529
Sixty times half equals thirty times one.

67
00:02:57,529 --> 00:03:00,057
Both torques halve, so they still match.

68
00:03:00,707 --> 00:03:01,808
Side by side.

69
00:03:01,808 --> 00:03:05,051
Two equal, opposite pushes
along the same line.

70
00:03:05,051 --> 00:03:07,662
The forces cancel, and nothing turns.

71
00:03:08,062 --> 00:03:11,035
Now move them apart, so
their lines do not meet.

72
00:03:11,035 --> 00:03:14,407
The forces still cancel, but
together they turn the body.

73
00:03:14,407 --> 00:03:16,235
This pair is called a couple.

74
00:03:16,835 --> 00:03:17,735
Your turn.

75
00:03:17,612 --> 00:03:20,182
Aarav turns a steering
wheel with a couple.

76
00:03:20,182 --> 00:03:22,476
Does the wheel also move off sideways?

77
00:03:22,476 --> 00:03:23,618
Pause and decide.

78
00:03:26,818 --> 00:03:27,718
No!

79
00:03:27,272 --> 00:03:30,466
The two forces cancel,
so the centre stays put.

80
00:03:30,466 --> 00:03:32,398
A couple only turns a body.

81
00:03:33,048 --> 00:03:34,738
Now, one worked example.

82
00:03:35,088 --> 00:03:39,584
A light rod, one metre long, hangs
from two strings at its ends.

83
00:03:39,584 --> 00:03:44,294
A sixty newton load hangs zero point
two five metres from the left end.

84
00:03:44,294 --> 00:03:45,907
Find the two tensions.

85
00:03:46,407 --> 00:03:50,717
Take torques about the left end,
so the left string has no torque.

86
00:03:50,717 --> 00:03:54,919
The right tension times one equals
sixty times zero point two five.

87
00:03:55,669 --> 00:03:57,629
So the right tension is fifteen newtons.

88
00:03:58,379 --> 00:04:00,453
The two tensions add up to sixty.

89
00:04:00,453 --> 00:04:02,983
So the left tension is forty five newtons.

90
00:04:03,733 --> 00:04:05,923
The string closer to
the load carries more.

91
00:04:06,573 --> 00:04:08,037
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:04:08,387 --> 00:04:12,515
Many students think a body is in
equilibrium as soon as the forces add

93
00:04:12,515 --> 00:04:13,415
to zero.

94
00:04:13,639 --> 00:04:14,539
Not enough.

95
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:18,022
A couple has zero total
force, yet it turns the body.

96
00:04:18,022 --> 00:04:19,744
You need zero torque too.

97
00:04:20,394 --> 00:04:21,879
Where is this asked?

98
00:04:21,879 --> 00:04:26,074
In planks on two supports, see-saws
and levers, and in couples.

99
00:04:26,674 --> 00:04:27,788
Exam level now.

100
00:04:27,788 --> 00:04:32,346
A uniform four metre plank weighing two
hundred newtons rests on two supports,

101
00:04:32,346 --> 00:04:34,394
each half a metre from an end.

102
00:04:34,394 --> 00:04:38,141
A six hundred newton man stands
one metre from the left end.

103
00:04:38,141 --> 00:04:40,378
Find the push of the left support.

104
00:04:45,778 --> 00:04:46,943
The answer is A.

105
00:04:46,943 --> 00:04:48,233
Six hundred newtons.

106
00:04:48,733 --> 00:04:52,422
Take torques about the right support,
three and a half metres from the

107
00:04:52,422 --> 00:04:53,322
left end.

108
00:04:53,605 --> 00:04:58,046
The left push times three equals two
hundred times one point five, plus

109
00:04:58,046 --> 00:05:00,286
six hundred times two point five.

110
00:05:00,286 --> 00:05:01,495
Eighteen hundred.

111
00:05:01,995 --> 00:05:04,665
So the left push is
eighteen hundred over three.

112
00:05:04,665 --> 00:05:05,862
Six hundred newtons.

113
00:05:05,862 --> 00:05:08,399
The right support takes
the other two hundred.

114
00:05:08,999 --> 00:05:10,539
Here is your recap.

115
00:05:10,539 --> 00:05:12,122
Screenshot this card.

116
00:05:15,322 --> 00:05:17,261
Can you answer these without help?

117
00:05:17,661 --> 00:05:18,561
One.

118
00:05:18,157 --> 00:05:22,212
What two conditions must a rigid
body meet to be in equilibrium?

119
00:05:23,112 --> 00:05:24,012
Two.

120
00:05:23,511 --> 00:05:26,134
Why must the heavy friend
sit closer to the pivot?

121
00:05:27,034 --> 00:05:27,934
Three.

122
00:05:27,587 --> 00:05:31,263
Why is a couple not in equilibrium,
though its forces cancel?

123
00:05:32,163 --> 00:05:35,377
Next time: a cardboard
cut-out of a strange shape.

124
00:05:35,377 --> 00:05:37,907
Where is the one point it balances on?

125
00:05:37,907 --> 00:05:39,187
Centre of gravity.
